// games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_scene_proof.emp — POISON (see README).
//
// TARGETS: `cfg_eq()` in games/sonic4/test/scene_equiv_proof.emp — the field-wise
// comparator the permanent equivalence witness is built on (spec §8.1).
// EXPECTED FRAGMENT: "differs at cfg field 3 (band field -1)"
// (It was field 4 until 2026-08-27: `pcfg_layer_mask` became a u16 and took byte $02,
//  which shifted every index above 1 down one place. The number is quoted on purpose —
//  see below — so it moves whenever the record does.)
//
// WHAT THIS POISON CAN AND CANNOT DO. The witness's own twenty `ensure`s live in a module
// no poison may edit, and they are already exercised red-first on an ordinary
// `DEBUG=1 ./build.sh` (Task 7 flipped five different expected fields and read five
// different indices back). What is NOT covered there is the comparator itself under the
// lane: a `cfg_eq` that returned -1 unconditionally would make all twenty proofs pass and
// nothing would notice. So this module builds its own miniature proof out of the REAL
// comparators, imported — never re-implemented — and perturbs the oracle by ONE FIELD.
//
// ONE FIELD, AND THE FRAGMENT PINS WHICH. The fixture scene declares `v_center: 512`; the
// oracle `hdr()` call is transcribed correctly in every argument EXCEPT `v_center: 256`.
// `pcfg_v_center_y` is field 3 (the index table at the top of scene_equiv_proof.emp), so a
// working comparator must report exactly 3 — and the lane's expected fragment quotes the
// number. That is not decoration. `cfg_eq` tests fields in REVERSE order so the lowest
// differing index survives, which means any additional mismatch at a HIGHER index would be
// masked by field 3 and this poison would go red for a reason that looks identical to the
// intended one. The control build below is what closes that hole, and the band half closes
// the rest of it.
//
// THE BAND HALF IS THE PASSING SIDE, and it is why `band_field -1` is in the fragment. The
// band oracle is transcribed CORRECTLY, so `band_eq()` must return -1: a comparator that
// reported a difference between two identical band entries would change that number and
// the case would fail. Both halves are bound to consts and reported by ONE ensure, so this
// poison names one guard and costs one diagnostic while proving the comparator can both
// find a difference and not invent one.
//
// `band_eq` TAKES TWO ARGUMENTS. The plan sketched `band_eq(a, b, i)`; Task 7 dropped the
// index, which has no work to do inside a field-wise compare and belongs in the message,
// where it is already a literal. This poison trips the two-argument shape, so a future
// signature change fails here as well as at the witness.
//
// THE ORACLE'S FREE NAMES RESOLVE HERE. `hdr()`'s body reads `PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE`,
// `MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS` and `RASTER_MAX_PATCH` — a comptime fn's free names resolve at the
// CALL SITE — and its `anchor_ch` DEFAULT is `PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE`. Left unimported, that
// default would degrade silently and the oracle would differ at field 8 (pcfg_anchor_ch)
// as well as field 3, which field 3 would then mask. All three are named below for that
// reason.
//
// TASK 9 LANDED: `hdr` and `cfg_band` moved out of games/sonic4/data/parallax/configs.emp
// (deleted with the whole directory) and into scene_equiv_proof.emp, so this module's two
// oracle imports are now one. Nothing else here changed.
module games.sonic4.test.poison.poison_scene_proof

use engine.constants.{MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS, PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE}
use engine.effects.raster_dsl.{RASTER_MAX_PATCH}
use engine.level.scene_dsl.*
use engine.level.parallax_dsl.*
use games.sonic4.scene_equiv_proof.{cfg_eq, band_eq, hdr, cfg_band}

// The fixture: one layer, no attachments, whole-plane V-scroll. Deliberately the simplest
// scene the model admits, so every oracle default below is the constructor's own default
// and the ONLY authored disagreement is the one this poison plants.
//
// P3 TASK 7: the top is `world_y: 512`, not 0, and that is not a value tweak. `world_y`
// is an ACT coordinate now, and this fixture's `v_center: 512 / v_factor: 3` maps it to
// plane line `(512 - 512) >> 3 = 0` — the same band the oracle's `cfg_band(cell: 0)`
// names. A `world_y: 0` here would sit ABOVE v_center, which has no plane image at all,
// and the two scene_plane_line() guards would fire and add two diagnostics the lane's
// count check cannot tell apart from the planted one.
const POISON_PROOF_SCENE: Scene = scene(
    layers: [ layer(world_y: 512, fa: FACTOR_1, fb: FACTOR_1_2, dsa: 15, dsb: 15),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(),
              no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(), no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer(),
              no_layer() ],
    count: 1, v_factor: 3, v_center: 512, v_offset: 0)

// THE PLANTED FIELD: `v_center: 256` against the fixture's 512 — pcfg_v_center_y, field 3.
const POISON_PROOF_H = cfg_eq(scene_hdr(POISON_PROOF_SCENE),
    hdr(band_count: 1, layer_mask: $01, v_factor_bg: 3, v_center: 256, v_offset: 0))
// The correct half: an identical band entry, which `band_eq` must call equal (-1).
const POISON_PROOF_B = band_eq(scene_band(POISON_PROOF_SCENE, 0),
    cfg_band(cell: 0, fa: FACTOR_1, fb: FACTOR_1_2, dsa: 15, dsb: 15))

ensure(POISON_PROOF_H == -1 && POISON_PROOF_B == -1,
       "poison_scene_proof: the one-field-off oracle differs at cfg field {POISON_PROOF_H} (band field {POISON_PROOF_B}) — the header oracle plants v_center 256 against the fixture's 512, which is pcfg_v_center_y (field 3); the band oracle is transcribed correctly and must stay -1. Any other pair of numbers means cfg_eq/band_eq is reporting something other than the planted difference")
